=== task: mcq medium
title: Why infinity?
prompt: Why does the coin table start every amount except 0 at `float("inf")`?
* Infinity means "can't be made yet": any real count is smaller, so the first way found replaces it, and an amount no coin reaches stays infinite :: Right. And infinity plus 1 is still infinity, so an impossible amount never spreads a finite count.
- Because Python lists can't hold None :: Lists can hold None, but `min` and `<` don't work with None and numbers.
- To make the table run faster :: The starting value doesn't change how many steps the loops take.
- Because infinity is the answer for amounts made of many coins :: Any amount that can be made has a finite count.
hint: What should happen the first time a way to make an amount is found?
hint: What is infinity plus 1?
right: Infinity is a starting value every real answer beats, and adding 1 to it changes nothing, so impossible amounts stay impossible and stand out at the end.
wrong: Starting at infinity makes the first real count win, keeps unreachable amounts infinite, and lets you return −1 for them.

